{
  "esm": "0.8.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "fv_laplacian_cell",
    "description": "Finite-volume cell Laplacian of a cell scalar on the MPAS Voronoi grid (the TRiSK discrete Laplacian, Ringler et al. 2010, the div-of-grad composition of eqs. 21-22): lap_i = (1/A_i) * sum_{k=1..nEdgesOnCell(i)} edgeSignOnCell(i,k) * dvEdge(e) * (phi(cellsOnEdge(e,2)) - phi(cellsOnEdge(e,1))) / dcEdge(e) with e = edgesOnCell(i,k) -- the exact Gauss/divergence theorem on cell i applied to the edge-normal gradient of phi, i.e. div(grad phi) with the flux integral along each edge approximated by the midpoint rule. This rule is the mimetic COMPOSITION of grids/mpas/rules/fv_divergence_cell with grids/mpas/rules/fv_gradient_edge: the divergence's per-edge flux F(e) is replaced INLINE by the two-point edge-normal gradient grad_e = (phi(c2) - phi(c1)) / dcEdge(e) that fv_gradient_edge would emit (c1 = cellsOnEdge(e,1), c2 = cellsOnEdge(e,2); the positive edge normal points c1 -> c2, the MPAS orientation shared by edgeSignOnCell and by both parent rules), so the summand is the outward-signed edge-normal gradient flux edgeSignOnCell(i,k)*dvEdge(e)*grad_e and the sum is the net flux through cell i's boundary, per unit area A_i. Emitting the composed one-shot stencil (rather than lowering div and grad on separate fixpoint passes) is the open-namespace option esm-spec 9.6.8 explicitly grants: 'A rule author MAY instead match laplacian directly and emit a one-shot stencil.' Matches the open-namespace rewrite-target {op: laplacian, args: [phi]} (esm-spec 9.6.8, 4.2: laplacian is author sugar with no evaluable core -- laplacian(u) = sum_i D(D(u,x_i),x_i) -- and a rule MAY match it directly on the bare op and emit a scheme rather than routing through the nested-D identity), match-scoped with `where: {phi: {shape: [cells]}}` (esm-spec 9.6.1) so it fires ONLY on the Laplacian of a bare field declared over this grid's `cells` index set -- the axis-less laplacian pattern would otherwise fire on every laplacian in a consuming component. The shape constraint is the mechanism that lets two MPAS meshes coexist in one document: import this rule twice under distinct index-set renames (esm-spec 9.7.7), and each instance's `where` shape follows its renamed cell set (e.g. [meshA.cells] vs [meshB.cells]), so each laplacian lowers by its own mesh's rule with no first-declared-wins tie-break. Because the constraint requires a bare variable of exactly that shape, a consumer that takes the Laplacian of a COMPOUND inline cell expression (e.g. laplacian(a*b)) must first bind it to a declared cell-shaped observed. The body is a single aggregate over the `cells` axis whose contracted index k runs over the RAGGED index set edges_of_cell (esm-spec 4.3.1 { from, of }; RFC semiring-faq-unified-ir 5.2): per-cell dynamic bound k <= nEdgesOnCell(i) plus a gather through the edgesOnCell values factor -- the same connectivity-based stencil form as fv_divergence_cell, since the Laplacian IS a divergence (its output is a cell aggregate contracting the cell's ragged edge ring), only with the edge flux built in-place from phi and dcEdge instead of read from a declared edge field. The division by areaCell(i) sits inside the summand, which equals (1/A_i)*sum since A_i is constant over k, exactly as in fv_divergence_cell. There are no boundary regions and no makearray because a closed sphere has no boundary: every edge separates exactly two cells, so every cellsOnEdge(e,1)/cellsOnEdge(e,2) gather and every edgeSignOnCell entry the ragged bound reaches is well defined (bc:none; a rule for a limited-area MPAS mesh would instead overwrite the boundary-cell entries -- one-sided/Neumann/Dirichlet closure of the edges with only one interior cell -- and would be a different rule). The free names edgeSignOnCell, edgesOnCell, dvEdge, cellsOnEdge, dcEdge, areaCell are the grid's keyed-factor contract (see grids/mpas/grid.esm): the consuming model must expose them as bare-name arrays, normally as observed aliases of a grids/mpas/mesh/ subsystem's const data (problems/laplacian_mpas.esm is the pattern) -- the union of fv_divergence_cell's factors (edgeSignOnCell, edgesOnCell, dvEdge, areaCell) and fv_gradient_edge's factors (cellsOnEdge, dcEdge), since the composed stencil needs both the divergence's cell-boundary connectivity and the gradient's flanking-cell gather. Accuracy: exactly ANNIHILATES spatially-constant fields (phi(c2)-phi(c1) = 0 for phi = const at every edge, hence every summand edgeSignOnCell*dvEdge*0/dcEdge = 0 and lap_i = 0 at every cell to rounding -- the Laplacian's null-space mimetic property, the discrete statement that laplacian(const) = 0; problems/laplacian_mpas.esm asserts it). Exactly CONSERVATIVE for any cell field phi (sum_i A_i*lap_i = 0: each interior edge e appears in exactly its two cells cellsOnEdge(e,1) and cellsOnEdge(e,2) with opposite edgeSignOnCell and the same dvEdge*grad_e, so the two cells' contributions cancel and the global integral of the discrete Laplacian is zero -- the discrete divergence-theorem closure inherited from fv_divergence_cell; problems/laplacian_mpas.esm asserts it for a non-constant field). And SYMMETRIC NEGATIVE-SEMIDEFINITE under the TRiSK inner products <a,b>_cells = sum_i areaCell(i)*a_i*b_i and <u,v>_edges = sum_e dvEdge(e)*dcEdge(e)*u_e*v_e: because lap = div(grad) and fv_gradient_edge is the negative discrete adjoint of fv_divergence_cell (grad = -div^T under those inner products, the closed-sphere summation-by-parts identity of section 3), the Laplacian satisfies <phi, lap psi>_cells = -sum_e dvEdge(e)*dcEdge(e)*grad(phi)_e*grad(psi)_e, which is symmetric in phi<->psi and equals -||grad phi||^2 <= 0 at psi = phi -- the discrete Dirichlet energy, the property that makes the TRiSK Laplacian a stable diffusion operator. The order tag is the guaranteed first-order bound on arbitrarily distorted Voronoi meshes, though the composed midpoint flux is second-order on centroidal/symmetric meshes where the edge midpoint bisects the cell-center arc (Ringler et al. 2010, section 3.2). A convergence-order sweep on refined MPAS meshes is a possible follow-up, out of scope for the exactness gate that ships with this rule.",
    "license": "MIT",
    "tags": ["esd:rule", "family:finite_volume", "grid:mpas", "op:laplacian", "axes:cells", "order:1", "bc:none", "spec:9.6.8"],
    "references": [
      {
        "citation": "Ringler, T., Thuburn, J., Klemp, J. B., & Skamarock, W. C. (2010). A unified approach to energy conservation and potential vorticity dynamics for arbitrarily-structured C-grids. Journal of Computational Physics, 229(9), 3065-3090. Eqs. (21)-(22) and the discrete div-grad adjoint (grad = -div^T) / Dirichlet-energy identities of section 3."
      },
      {
        "citation": "Thuburn, J., Ringler, T. D., Skamarock, W. C., & Klemp, J. B. (2009). Numerical representation of geostrophic modes on arbitrarily structured C-grids. Journal of Computational Physics, 228(22), 8321-8335."
      }
    ]
  },
  "expression_template_imports": [
    { "ref": "../grid.esm" }
  ],
  "expression_templates": {
    "fv_laplacian_cell": {
      "params": ["phi"],
      "match": { "op": "laplacian", "args": ["phi"] },
      "where": { "phi": { "shape": ["cells"] } },
      "body": {
        "op": "aggregate",
        "args": ["phi", "edgeSignOnCell", "edgesOnCell", "dvEdge", "cellsOnEdge", "dcEdge", "areaCell"],
        "output_idx": ["i"],
        "semiring": "sum_product",
        "ranges": {
          "i": { "from": "cells" },
          "k": { "from": "edges_of_cell", "of": ["i"] }
        },
        "expr": {
          "op": "/",
          "args": [
            {
              "op": "*",
              "args": [
                { "op": "index", "args": ["edgeSignOnCell", "i", "k"] },
                { "op": "index", "args": ["dvEdge", { "op": "index", "args": ["edgesOnCell", "i", "k"] }] },
                {
                  "op": "/",
                  "args": [
                    {
                      "op": "-",
                      "args": [
                        { "op": "index", "args": ["phi", { "op": "index", "args": ["cellsOnEdge", { "op": "index", "args": ["edgesOnCell", "i", "k"] }, 2] }] },
                        { "op": "index", "args": ["phi", { "op": "index", "args": ["cellsOnEdge", { "op": "index", "args": ["edgesOnCell", "i", "k"] }, 1] }] }
                      ]
                    },
                    { "op": "index", "args": ["dcEdge", { "op": "index", "args": ["edgesOnCell", "i", "k"] }] }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            { "op": "index", "args": ["areaCell", "i"] }
          ]
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
